     "result_text_tail": " around month 26 with about $4.9M left; below roughly 35% growth it doesn't work within current cash. This projection is illustrative: it assumes costs stay flat apart from the new hires, which is optimistic.\n\n**The deck also covers:**\n- The market and the incumbents.\n- The customer evidence (survey answers versus who actually kept ordering).\n- Three further risks: rider minimum pay, competing with Parcelbird's own customers, and distraction from the core business.\n- A scorecard of three options.\n- What evidence would reopen the question.\n- An appendix with the full calculations and assumptions.\n\n**Choices I made where you'd normally be asked:**\n- **The $9.5M:** the pack doesn't say whether it includes today's $0.4M a month spending on the software business, so I show both readings. The recommendation holds either way.\n- **Runway test:** I measured the 12-month rule at today's spending, which is the most favourable test for the founders, and assumed their spending is even over 18 months.\n- **A middle option:** I added a one- or two-store test of Parcelbird's own to compare against. It fails too.\n- **Competing with customers:** this risk is labelled as my judgement, since the pack has no customer feedback on it.\n- **Format:** PowerPoint only, with no PDF, because I can't produce a faithful PDF in this sandbox.\n\nFiles are in `deliverable/`:\n- Parcelbird_Now_board_paper_Jan2027.pptx\n- README.md (storyline and the choices above)\n\nThe scripts that rebuild the deck are in `build/`."
    }
